From: OCCTSwift ecosystem <noreply@secondmouse.au>
Subject: [PATCH] Modeling Data - GProp_SelGProps and GProp_VelGProps compute
 the matrix of inertia of a gp_Cylinder, gp_Sphere and gp_Torus wrongly, and the
 centre of mass of the solid, and the area and volume of a torus over part of the
 tube

GProp_SelGProps::Perform and GProp_VelGProps::Perform for gp_Cylinder, gp_Sphere and
gp_Torus build Dm, the matrix of inertia about the location of the surface in its own
axes, from expressions that do not integrate to anything, then assemble inertia from it
the way the gp_Cone overloads did before 0055: as gp_Mat(rows of lambda_i * v_i) from
the Jacobi decomposition of Dm, which is diag(lambda) V^T and not V diag(lambda) V^T, so
the sign Jacobi happens to give an eigenvector changes the matrix, with Dm, which is about
the location, added to the Huyghens term of the centre of mass as if it were already
about the centre of mass. The patch computes every entry of Dm as an integral and
assembles inertia = P Dm P^T - H(g, Location) + H(g, loc), with P the columns of the axes
of the surface and H the GProp::HOperator term, exactly as 0055 does for gp_Cone.

With the angular integrals over u in [a1, a2] (Cc = int cos^2 u, Ss = int sin^2 u,
Cs = int sin u cos u, C1 = int cos u, S1 = int sin u) the entries, per overload, are:

  gp_Cylinder, surface (x = R cos u, y = R sin u, dA = R du dz)
    xx = R^3 (Z2 - Z1) Cc   yy = R^3 (Z2 - Z1) Ss   xy = R^3 (Z2 - Z1) Cs
    zz = R (a2 - a1) (Z2^3 - Z1^3) / 3
    xz = R^2 (Z2^2 - Z1^2) / 2 C1   yz = R^2 (Z2^2 - Z1^2) / 2 S1
  gp_Cylinder, volume (the solid between the axis and the patch, dV = rho drho du dz)
    the same with R^4 / 4 and R^2 / 2 and R^3 / 3 in place of R^3, R and R^2, and the
    centre of mass at 2 R / 3 times the mean unit vector, not R times it
  gp_Sphere, surface (P = R (cos v cos u, cos v sin u, sin v), dA = R^2 cos v dv du)
    xx = R^4 Cc int cos^3 v   yy = R^4 Ss int cos^3 v   xy = R^4 Cs int cos^3 v
    zz = R^4 (a2 - a1)(sin^3 v2 - sin^3 v1) / 3
    xz = R^4 C1 (cos^3 v1 - cos^3 v2) / 3   yz = the same with S1
  gp_Sphere, volume (dV = t^2 R^3 cos v dt dv du): the same with R^5 / 5 and the centre
    of mass at 3 R / 4 times the mean unit vector in x and y and 3 R (sin v2 + sin v1) / 8
    in z, where the code had R and R (sin v2 + sin v1) / 2
  gp_Torus, surface (rho = R + r cos v, dA = rho r dv du)
    the area is r (R (v2 - v1) + r (sin v2 - sin v1)) (u2 - u1), which a part of the tube
    exposes, where the code read R r (u2 - u1) (v2 - v1)
    xx, yy, xy = r Cc, Ss, Cs int rho^3 dv   xz, yz = r^2 C1, S1 int rho^2 sin v dv
    zz = r^3 (u2 - u1) int sin^2 v rho dv
    the centre of mass is int rho^2 / int rho in x and y, not the mean of rho
  gp_Torus, volume (the solid swept by the segment from the circle through the centres of
  the tube to the patch, P = ((R + t r cos v) cos u, (R + t r cos v) sin u, t r sin v),
  dV = (R + t r cos v) t r^2 dt dv du)
    the volume is r^2 (R (v2 - v1) / 2 + r (sin v2 - sin v1) / 3) (u2 - u1), which is
    R r^2 (u2 - u1)(v2 - v1) / 2 over a full turn about the tube, the one the code reads

Against those the code has these defects, all in the six overloads:

  - The inertia assembly above, in all six, and Dm itself: the cylinder's Dm(3, 3) is
    Alpha2 - Alpha1 where the second moment about the axis is R^3 (Z2 - Z1) (Alpha2 -
    Alpha1), so for the full gp_Cylinder surface of radius 5 and height 10 it reads
    314.159 where the integral gives 7853.98, and its xz and yz entries drop a factor R.
  - GProp_VelGProps, gp_Cylinder: the centre of mass of a partial turn uses R times the
    mean unit vector, where the sector of a disc has 2 R / 3 times it.
  - GProp_VelGProps, gp_Sphere: x and y use R, not 3 R / 4, and z uses R (sin v2 +
    sin v1) / 2, not 3 R (sin v2 + sin v1) / 8, so even a full turn reads wrong in z for
    a part of the latitude.
  - GProp_VelGProps, gp_Torus: Cnt2 is cos(Alpha2) where cos(Teta2) is meant, so the
    angular integrals of the second sine and cosine are wrong whenever Teta2 differs from
    Alpha2.
  - gp_Torus, both: the mass over part of the tube, and the centre of mass, as above.

The mass and the centre of mass of the gp_Cylinder surface and of the gp_Sphere surface,
and the mass of the gp_Cylinder and gp_Sphere volumes, were already right and are not
touched.

The convention for the gp_Torus volume is the one that makes its full-range value the
volume of the solid torus, 2 pi^2 R r^2, which the code already reads, and that matches
the gp_Cylinder, gp_Sphere and gp_Cone overloads, which sweep the solid from the axis or
the centre to the patch: here from the circle through the centres of the tube. OCCT does
not say what a part of the tube means for this class. GProp_SelGProps and
GProp_VelGProps have no caller anywhere in the OCCT tree, so there is no call site to
copy, and the arbiter is an independent Gauss-Legendre integral over the parameter space,
written in the probe, which shares no formula with the code above.

Not touched: Perform stores g in global coordinates, where CentreOfMass() and
MatrixOfInertia() read it relative to loc (and GProp_GProps::Add reads it as loc + g), so
both are right only when loc is the origin. That inconsistency is older than 0055, is the
same in every Perform of both classes and the cone's, and is a separate change.

Measured on macOS arm64 against V8_0_1 with 0050, 0051 and 0055 applied and the two
translation units override-linked: 96 checks (the cylinder, sphere and torus; the surface
and the volume; four ranges each, full and partial in u and v; the frame of the surface
untilted and tilted and translated; mass, centre of mass, matrix of inertia about it, and
the moment about an oblique axis through a point away from the origin). 96 fail before and
0 after. Largest entry of the matrix of inertia relative to the integral, before and
after: cylinder surface 4.4 and 2.6e-14, volume 13 and 1.1e-13; sphere surface 3.4 and
4.4e-14, volume 5.2 and 5.2e-14; torus surface 2.5 and 8e-15, volume 2.3 and 2.2e-14. The
largest centre-of-mass distance after the patch is 1.4e-13, and before it 2.1 (cylinder
volume), 1.0 (sphere volume), 1.2 and 2.8 (torus). Torus mass over part of the tube, before:
off by 81% (surface) and 43% (volume); after, exact.

The reproducer is Scripts/repro/3091/probe.cxx, run by Scripts/repro/3091/run.sh, with the
transcript in Scripts/repro/3091/transcript.txt, in the OCCTSwift repository. OCCTSwift
issue #3091. It applies on top of 0050, 0051 and 0055 and not without them, since the two
files it changes are the ones they change.

